<section image-layout="blank" id="qPTo9Ra8PL" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="j19AMP_Atx" vertical-align="center" colspan="35"><p role="eyebrow">History detective case · Southern Brazil · 1835–1845</p><display size="lg">Three clues.</display><h1 font-size="42pt">One unfinished story.</h1><p font-size="lg"><b>Tax paper · folded map · letter from home</b></p><p font-size="lg">What can they reveal about the Ragamuffin War, known in Portuguese as the <i>Revolução Farroupilha</i>?</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="V4cMI6-6I3" background-color="accent" padding="14pt 18pt"><p role="eyebrow">Detective question</p><h3>Why did it begin—and why did different lives feel it differently?</h3></grid-item></grid></grid-item><grid-item id="B2xnv6g21v" padding="0" colspan="25"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/Yaffpv4CNHlZ4_f4Cqm8R.png" saved-media-id="41xc7hqhvpiodrl" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum-table illustration of three carefully arranged objects from nineteenth-century southern Brazil: a blank tax paper with wax seal, a folded schematic map with no lettering, and a tied letter from home, colored-pencil shading on warm cream paper, postcard red question marks as small paper cutouts, river blue and sun yellow accents, clear daylight, no people, no weapons, no legible text" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="M1mbzey5q6"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="KNd-D_3P6d"><h2>First clue: where did it happen?</h2><p font-size="lg"><b>South America → Brazil → Rio Grande do Sul</b></p><grid marker="number" marker-size="large" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" spacing="medium" padding-top="30pt"><grid-item id="sE8gWuhsoa" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h3>South America</h3><p font-size="18pt">Brazil is one country on this continent.</p><custom-code><style>.rs-south-america{height:9.2em;width:100%;font-family:var(--body-font)}.rs-south-america .land{fill:#2D7180;stroke:var(--body-color);stroke-width:2;stroke-linejoin:round}.rs-south-america .focus{fill:#E2A72F;stroke:#C9473D;stroke-width:3}</style><div class="rs-south-america"><svg width="100%" height="100%" viewBox="0 0 240 150" role="img" aria-label="Schematic outline of South America with Brazil highlighted"><path class="land" d="M88 7 L126 14 L151 32 L145 51 L158 68 L145 86 L132 95 L123 120 L105 144 L91 124 L84 101 L68 86 L61 67 L70 49 L57 34 L72 19 Z"></path><path class="focus" d="M94 31 L124 28 L143 42 L140 61 L150 72 L136 88 L121 92 L109 78 L92 73 L82 56 Z"></path></svg></div></custom-code></grid-item><grid-item id="a4et7KRvbQ" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h3>Brazil</h3><p font-size="18pt">Now look toward the far south.</p><custom-code><style>.rs-brazil{height:9.2em;width:100%;font-family:var(--body-font)}.rs-brazil .land{fill:#E2A72F;stroke:var(--body-color);stroke-width:2;stroke-linejoin:round}.rs-brazil .focus{fill:#C9473D;stroke:var(--body-color);stroke-width:2}</style><div class="rs-brazil"><svg width="100%" height="100%" viewBox="0 0 240 150" role="img" aria-label="Schematic outline of Brazil with the far south highlighted"><path class="land" d="M72 13 L121 8 L153 24 L170 50 L160 70 L173 88 L149 106 L136 136 L108 146 L97 123 L79 109 L70 85 L50 62 L59 38 Z"></path><path class="focus" d="M108 118 L136 111 L136 136 L108 146 L98 126 Z"></path></svg></div></custom-code></grid-item><grid-item id="Bb6d5AOL7f" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h3>Rio Grande do Sul</h3><p font-size="18pt">The conflict was centered here.</p><custom-code><style>.rs-state{height:9.2em;width:100%;font-family:var(--body-font)}.rs-state .land{fill:#2D7180;stroke:var(--body-color);stroke-width:2;stroke-linejoin:round}.rs-state .coast{fill:none;stroke:#E2A72F;stroke-width:6;stroke-linecap:round}.rs-state .pin{fill:#C9473D;stroke:#FFF9E8;stroke-width:4}</style><div class="rs-state"><svg width="100%" height="100%" viewBox="0 0 240 150" role="img" aria-label="Schematic outline of Rio Grande do Sul with a location marker"><path class="land" d="M48 34 L128 20 L174 47 L166 91 L137 128 L78 121 L42 84 Z"></path><path class="coast" d="M174 47 Q179 72 166 91 Q151 112 137 128"></path><circle class="pin" cx="112" cy="76" r="13"></circle></svg></div></custom-code></grid-item></grid><p role="caption"><b>Map note:</b> Modern location guide; outlines are schematic, not exact historical borders. · Source: <a href="https://www.rs.gov.br/carta-de-servicos/servicos?servico=2268">Rio Grande do Sul government</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="3qEGBRvkCe" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="OsBjJOLiBY"><h2>Two objects reveal two kinds of disagreement</h2><p font-size="lg"><b>There was not one simple cause—and people wanted different political futures.</b></p><grid divider="3pt palette-3" image-aspect="landscape" image-shape="rounded"><grid-item id="wRLGkSjpK5" colspan="17"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/XQTLd20C7RL_p4c-gMTTi.png" saved-media-id="p3cnn68vgkswyyq" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum illustration of a nineteenth-century tax paper beside plain coins and a red seal, with no legible text"><h3>Trade and taxes</h3><p font-size="18pt">Who pays? Who sets the trade rules?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="53M3K6Ds0O" colspan="17"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/3PnuHsD5EjGkp4xI6QlOm.png" saved-media-id="2rh0htpgbi8hp0l" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum illustration of a folded schematic regional map with no labels"><h3>Regional power</h3><p font-size="18pt">How much say should Rio Grande do Sul have?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="iAiDcYVlfk" colspan="26"><p role="eyebrow">Different political futures</p><h3>The argument pulled in two directions</h3><grid direction="vertical" spacing="small" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="9AS1N81jnj" background-color="accent" padding="14pt 18pt"><h4>Some rebels</h4><p font-size="18pt">Wanted a separate republic with its own government.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="6Cr2V9OkzX" background-color="palette-2" padding="14pt 18pt"><h4>Imperial government</h4><p font-size="18pt">Wanted Brazil to remain together.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid><p font-size="18pt"><b>Different people made different choices for different reasons.</b> No whole side was simply good or bad.</p><p role="caption">Source: <a href="https://cultura.rs.gov.br/upload/arquivos/carga20210405/27100547-anais-ahrs-v-16-1.pdf">Anais AHRS, vol. 16 (PDF), State Historical Archive of Rio Grande do Sul</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="c53f8YksRC"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="P4D3ip60K5"><h2>How long did the conflict last?</h2><p font-size="lg">From <b>1835</b> to <b>1845</b>: ten calendar years.</p><grid spacing="large"><grid-item id="vzLbvlv1Lm" background-color="palette-3" vertical-align="center" colspan="17"><display size="lg">10</display><h3>years</h3><p font-size="18pt">Longer than most children spend in primary school.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="n2poDhb5fu" vertical-align="center" colspan="43"><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="G3tXeUhrk0"><p role="eyebrow">1835 · begins</p></grid-item><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><grid-item id="C6fS797G7R"><p role="eyebrow">1845 · ends</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="2bVVOsnZx-" background-color="accent" padding="0" colspan="6"><p></p></grid-item><grid-item id="f3K9heo1JU" background-color="palette-3" padding="0" colspan="6"><p></p></grid-item><grid-item id="okMEx89pED" background-color="accent" padding="0" colspan="6"><p></p></grid-item><grid-item id="anOD09MQM6" background-color="palette-3" padding="0" colspan="6"><p></p></grid-item><grid-item id="F8POIF4szM" background-color="accent" padding="0" colspan="6"><p></p></grid-item><grid-item id="1h60hwrmYf" background-color="palette-3" padding="0" colspan="6"><p></p></grid-item><grid-item id="-wzxY0jhbJ" background-color="accent" padding="0" colspan="6"><p></p></grid-item><grid-item id="Oc-wJE1RgC" background-color="palette-3" padding="0" colspan="6"><p></p></grid-item><grid-item id="csKTEgIF9j" background-color="accent" padding="0" colspan="6"><p></p></grid-item><grid-item id="chXLGPnMTr" background-color="palette-3" padding="0" colspan="6"><p></p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="hX05Qq9-Cw" colspan="20"><h4>Start</h4><p font-size="18pt">Political conflict begins.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="FtnrnSzpzW" align="center" colspan="20"><h4 align="center">A long middle</h4><p font-size="18pt" align="center">Families and workers live through years of uncertainty.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="kciizvTgGq" align="right" colspan="20"><h4 align="right">End</h4><p font-size="18pt" align="right">The conflict stops in 1845.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="5LxkSH1FxO" background-color="surface" padding="12pt 18pt"><p font-size="18pt"><b>Duration clue:</b> The ruler shows how long the conflict continued; it does not invent events for the years in between.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption">Source: <a href="https://cultura.rs.gov.br/upload/arquivos/carga20210405/27100547-anais-ahrs-v-16-1.pdf">Anais AHRS, vol. 16 (PDF)</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="zWyPZoJcSu"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="aFWW1SrX26"><p role="eyebrow">Illustrated viewpoints—not real biographies</p><h2>The same years did not feel the same to everyone</h2><p font-size="lg"><b>People did not have equal rights or choices.</b></p><grid spacing="medium" image-aspect="square" image-shape="circle"><spacer span="2"></spacer><grid-item id="q6LQfu7ubs" colspan="14"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/gGD2C5OqPDa_aGW67kzph.png" saved-media-id="zfxsdimdmwxh2fq" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper and colored-pencil portrait of an illustrative free market seller named Rosa in 1830s–1840s southern Brazil, adult woman with medium-brown skin beside a simple produce basket and market cloth, historically grounded but non-specific clothing, restrained thoughtful expression, warm cream paper background, no text, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1440,&quot;height&quot;:1440}"><h4>Rosa</h4><p font-size="16pt"><b>Free market seller</b></p><p font-size="18pt">She watches trade rules change.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="0L8QreX9N2" colspan="14"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/lgxSyDvqSpL5nmoDRcVvx.png" saved-media-id="j9wxj1llgjnq80i" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper and colored-pencil portrait of an illustrative rural worker named Tomás in 1830s–1840s southern Brazil, adult man with tan skin beside a travel bag and food sack, historically grounded but non-specific work clothing, calm serious expression, warm cream paper background, no text, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1440,&quot;height&quot;:1440}"><h4>Tomás</h4><p font-size="16pt"><b>Rural worker</b></p><p font-size="18pt">Work, food, and travel shape his choices.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="fvvVFiQVdc" colspan="14"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/1g5c3NTv_hlHKIVrdYs4h.png" saved-media-id="faklibuhgmv1rgw" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper and colored-pencil portrait of an illustrative enslaved worker named Luzia in 1830s–1840s southern Brazil, adult Black woman standing with dignity beside folded cloth and a carrying basket, historically grounded but non-specific clothing, restrained direct expression, warm cream paper background, no chains, no violence, no text, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1440,&quot;height&quot;:1440}"><h4>Luzia</h4><p font-size="16pt"><b>Enslaved worker</b></p><p font-size="18pt">She has far fewer rights. Slavery continues after the revolt.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Ln_Vcd2hoJ" colspan="14"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/Eso7Aks0Yke-EPaifM4xy.png" saved-media-id="ev5nhqxkrdhzxps" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper and colored-pencil portrait of an illustrative child named Davi in 1830s–1840s southern Brazil, brown-skinned child listening while nearby adult hands hold an opened letter with no readable writing, historically grounded but non-specific clothing, curious restrained expression, warm cream paper background, no text, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1440,&quot;height&quot;:1440}"><h4>Davi</h4><p font-size="16pt"><b>Child</b></p><p font-size="18pt">Adults shape the news he hears.</p></grid-item><spacer span="2"></spacer></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="maVkVYE7pw" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="4d23GEQAGt"><p role="eyebrow">Illustrated object reconstructions</p><h2>Everyday objects carry big questions</h2><grid spacing="small" cell-size="10" image-aspect="landscape" image-shape="rounded"><grid-item id="wW773i3Qcl" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/XQTLd20C7RL_p4c-gMTTi.png" saved-media-id="p3cnn68vgkswyyq" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum illustration of a nineteenth-century tax paper on warm cream paper beside a few plain coins and a red seal, colored-pencil shading, postcard red and sun yellow accents, no people, no legible text, no logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><h4>Tax paper</h4><p>Who pays—and who decides the rules?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="_Og7SG9d30" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/3PnuHsD5EjGkp4xI6QlOm.png" saved-media-id="2rh0htpgbi8hp0l" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum illustration of a folded schematic map on warm cream paper, river-blue route lines and one postcard-red region marker, visibly folded paper edges, no exact borders, no labels, no legible text" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><h4>Map</h4><p>Who should govern the region?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="v39gtA3XYn" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/qvqut_qXqFkECEzKki72H.png" saved-media-id="rfp0asqdqgesx26" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum illustration of a tied letter from home opened beside a travel bag on warm cream paper, soft colored-pencil shading, river-blue ribbon and postcard-red wax seal, no readable writing, no people" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><h4>Letter</h4><p>How do people learn what is happening?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="IXQBsr8SKv" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/_oUAvg-7ZvT2Jxg1zW_n5.png" saved-media-id="p43967s4w8dgccc" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum illustration of a practical food basket holding bread, dried beans and simple produce on warm cream paper, colored-pencil shading with sun-yellow and river-blue cloth accents, ordinary household scale, no text, no people" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><h4>Food basket</h4><p>How do families keep daily life going?</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>Objects help us study political ideas and ordinary experiences together.</b></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="xMdwlYaDbP"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="8H1rDq98mq" vertical-align="end" padding="18pt" colspan="22"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/FkgXYT8UJUI9N3ZoID4Q9.png" saved-media-id="wsi7wj2v1atjszo" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum-table illustration of an opened folded map reconnected to a tax paper, a tied letter and a food basket by one winding postcard-red paper thread, warm cream ground, river-blue geography marks and sun-yellow discovery tabs, clear daylight, no people, no flags, no weapons, no readable text" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="FQfpf2qsQv" background-color="accent" padding="12pt 16pt"><p role="eyebrow">Illustrated reconstruction</p><h3>The clues reconnect</h3></grid-item></grid></grid-item><grid-item id="ZpCDqdc9Ob" colspan="38"><h2>1845 changed one thing—not everything</h2><grid direction="vertical" spacing="small" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="2i56RtlhqF" cell-style="subtle"><labels><label variant="solid">Changed</label></labels><h3>The conflict stopped</h3><p font-size="18pt">The ten-year conflict ended in 1845.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="VSx83LYBe6" cell-style="subtle"><labels><label variant="outline">Did not change</label></labels><p font-size="18pt"><b>Brazil stayed together.</b> Rio Grande do Sul did not remain separate.</p><p font-size="18pt"><b>Slavery continued.</b> Abolition was not an outcome of the revolt.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="sgVxkyKnSJ" background-color="accent" padding="14pt 18pt"><h4>Big idea</h4><p font-size="18pt">Ending a conflict does not automatically make life fair for everyone.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption">Source: <a href="https://cultura.rs.gov.br/upload/arquivos/carga20210405/27100547-anais-ahrs-v-16-1.pdf">Anais AHRS, vol. 16 (PDF)</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="DePWSpd22N" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="gQZ7gOjImT"><h2>Solve the case—then keep one question open</h2><p font-size="lg">Work in pairs. Use one clue from the map, objects, timeline, or viewpoints for each answer.</p><grid marker="number" marker-size="large" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" spacing="medium" padding-top="34pt"><grid-item id="LBC_Z0oFrn" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Find the place</h4><p font-size="18pt">Which Brazilian state is at the center of this story?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="e2ZRfMfON5" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Find the causes</h4><p font-size="18pt">Name two kinds of disagreement behind the conflict.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="dnLxQ_9zgh" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Compare viewpoints</h4><p font-size="18pt">Why might the same event feel different to a free seller and an enslaved worker?</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="NaetB0pnn8" background-color="accent" padding="16pt 20pt"><p role="eyebrow">The case is not completely closed</p><h3>A date can end a conflict. It cannot tell every story.</h3><p font-size="18pt"><b>Final detective question:</b> Whose voice might still be missing?</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid><notes><p>Quiz check: Rio Grande do Sul. Possible causes include trade and tax disputes, regional power, and disagreement over separation from Brazil. Viewpoints differed because people did not have equal rights or choices. Invite children to suggest other missing civilian voices without inventing biographies.</p></notes></section>